.TH GGE\-BUILD 7 "generalGameEngine" "Engine Manual" "Engine Manual"
.SH NAME
gge\-build \- project layout, CMake, engine options, gge.conf, and extending the engine
.SH DESCRIPTION
A game is a directory with a CMakeLists.txt, a src/ tree, an assets/ tree, and the engine checked out as a git submodule under vendored/engine. The engine builds as a static library together with SDL3 (and optionally SDL_image and SDL_ttf), and the game links it. \fBgge init\fR (gge(1)) creates all of this; this page explains what it made and how to change it.
.PP
.SH LAYOUT
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
my_game/
  CMakeLists.txt
  gge.conf                 project settings \-> src/gge_config.h (generated, not committed)
  .clangd                  points clangd at build/compile_commands.json
  src/
    main.c                 EngineConfig, engine_init/run/shutdown
    game_state.h           GameState, SAVE_VERSION, game_state_init
    scenes.h               one 'Scene *x_scene(void);' per scene
    scenes/scene_title.c   one file per scene
    scenes/scene_game.c
    entities/dog.h, dog.c  one pair per entity kind (gge entity)
    data.h                 static const tables (optional)
  assets/
    sprites/ tiles/ fonts/ sfx/ music/
  vendored/
    engine/                the engine submodule (never edited from the game)
      src/engine/<module>/ one folder per module
      tools/gge            this tool; tools/man/ these pages
      vendored/SDL, SDL_image, SDL_ttf
  build/                   CMake output (ignored)
.EE
.RE
.PP
Every .c under src/ is compiled into the game automatically (\fBfile(GLOB_RECURSE ... CONFIGURE_DEPENDS)\fR), so adding a scene or entity file needs no CMake edit; just build again. Headers are found by bare name from anywhere in src/ and from the engine (\fB#include "engine.h"\fR, \fB#include "dog.h"\fR).
.PP
.SH CMAKELISTS.TXT
The one \fBgge init\fR writes:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.21)
project(my_game LANGUAGES C)
set(CMAKE_C_STANDARD 17)
set(CMAKE_C_STANDARD_REQUIRED ON)
set(CMAKE_EXPORT_COMPILE_COMMANDS ON)
\&
set(ENGINE_WITH_IMAGE ON CACHE BOOL "" FORCE)     # PNG/JPG via SDL_image
set(ENGINE_WITH_TTF   ON CACHE BOOL "" FORCE)     # fonts via SDL_ttf
add_subdirectory(vendored/engine)
\&
add_custom_command(                               # gge.conf \-> src/gge_config.h
  OUTPUT  ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/src/gge_config.h
  COMMAND sh ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/vendored/engine/tools/gge config
          \-f ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/gge.conf \-e ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/src/gge_config.h
  DEPENDS ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/gge.conf)
\&
file(GLOB_RECURSE GAME_SOURCES CONFIGURE_DEPENDS ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/src/*.c)
add_executable(${PROJECT_NAME} ${GAME_SOURCES} ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/src/gge_config.h)
target_include_directories(${PROJECT_NAME} PRIVATE ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/src)
target_link_libraries(${PROJECT_NAME} PRIVATE engine)      # engine headers, SDL3, SDL_image, SDL_ttf
target_compile_options(${PROJECT_NAME} PRIVATE \-Wall \-Wextra)
\&
add_custom_command(TARGET ${PROJECT_NAME} POST_BUILD       # assets/ next to the executable
    COMMAND ${CMAKE_COMMAND} \-E copy_directory
            ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/assets $<TARGET_FILE_DIR:${PROJECT_NAME}>/assets)
.EE
.RE
.PP
The two \fBset(... CACHE BOOL "" FORCE)\fR lines must come before \fBadd_subdirectory(vendored/engine)\fR. Linking \fBengine\fR brings every engine module folder onto the include path and links SDL statically, so the executable has no .dll/.dylib/.so to ship.
.PP
.SH BUILDING
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
cmake \-S . \-B build \-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug      # once, and after changing CMakeLists or options
cmake \-\-build build \-j                            # every time
\&./build/my_game                                   # run from anywhere; assets are found next to the binary
.EE
.RE
.PP
The first build compiles SDL (a few minutes); later builds take seconds. \fB\-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release\fR turns on optimization and defines NDEBUG (ASSERT disappears). Ninja (\fB\-G Ninja\fR) is faster than Make when installed. Delete \fBbuild/\fR to start over.
.PP
Requirements: CMake 3.21+, a C17 compiler (clang or gcc; on Windows, MinGW\-w64 or MSVC through the Visual Studio generator), git. On Linux, SDL needs the X11/Wayland development packages listed in vendored/engine/vendored/SDL/docs/README\-linux.md. On macOS, Xcode command line tools suffice. MSVC does not understand the GCC\-style \fB\-Wall \-Wextra\fR flags (it warns and ignores them); wrap that line in \fBif(NOT MSVC)\fR for a clean Windows build.
.PP
.SH ENGINE OPTIONS
.TP
\fBENGINE_WITH_IMAGE\fR
builds SDL_image; assets_texture loads PNG/JPG. Off: BMP only.
.TP
\fBENGINE_WITH_TTF\fR
builds SDL_ttf with its bundled FreeType; text_load_font works. Off: every Font is the 8x8 debug font.
Both default to OFF inside the engine and are turned on from the game's CMakeLists (\fBgge init \-I \-T\fR writes them ON). Changing an option requires re\-running the configure step. The matching submodule must be checked out: \fBgit submodule update \-\-init \-\-recursive\fR from the game root fetches SDL, SDL_image and SDL_ttf through the engine.
.PP
.SH GGE.CONF
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
title=My Game
logical_w=640
logical_h=360
window_scale=2
tick_rate=60
tile=16
org=cal
app=my_game
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBgge config\fR turns it into src/gge_config.h as \fBGGE_TITLE\fR, \fBGGE_LOGICAL_W\fR, ... (strings quoted, numbers bare), which main.c uses to fill EngineConfig. The build regenerates the header whenever gge.conf changes. \fBgge config tick_rate 120\fR edits a value; \fBgge config\fR alone prints them all. Add your own keys freely (\fBmax_lives=3\fR becomes \fBGGE_MAX_LIVES\fR). Do not edit or commit gge_config.h.
.PP
.SH THE ENGINE AS A SUBMODULE
\fBvendored/engine\fR is a git submodule pinned to a commit. To take a newer engine: \fBcd vendored/engine && git pull origin main && cd ../.. && git add vendored/engine && git commit \-m "engine update"\fR. Cloning a game elsewhere needs \fBgit clone \-\-recursive\fR, or \fBgit submodule update \-\-init \-\-recursive\fR afterwards. Fixes to the engine belong in the engine repository, committed there and then pulled into games; editing files under vendored/engine from a game leaves the game pointing at a dirty submodule.
.PP
.SH ADDING A MODULE TO THE ENGINE
Inside the engine repository, a module is a folder under src/engine/ with a header and, if needed, a .c file; the engine's CMakeLists globs every folder into the library and adds each as a public include directory, so nothing else changes:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
src/engine/particles/particles.h
src/engine/particles/particles.c
.EE
.RE
.PP
Conventions the existing modules follow:
.IP \(bu 2
One prefix per module (\fBparticles_\fR), a struct the caller owns (\fBParticles\fR), init/update/draw functions taking a pointer to it, no hidden global state unless the module is a singleton by nature (assets cache, audio device).
.IP \(bu 2
No SDL types in the public header where an engine type exists (Color, Rect, Texture, Input).
.IP \(bu 2
Failures log and return false/NULL/zero; nothing aborts.
.IP \(bu 2
Anything a game may need to save is pointer\-free; anything with pointers is documented as presentation state.
.IP \(bu 2
A \fB/** ... */\fR comment above every public prototype, struct and macro, with \fB@return\fR, \fB@field\fR and \fB@see\fR lines, then \fBgge genman\fR to regenerate the section\-3 pages and gge\-api(7); a section\-7 page in tools/man/man7/gge\-<module>.7 for how the pieces fit.
Game\-specific code stays in the game; the engine takes only what two games would both use.
.PP
.SH DEMOS INSIDE THE ENGINE
The engine's own CMakeLists builds \fBsrc/game/*.c\fR into an executable named \fBgame_engine\fR when such files exist, with \fBassets/\fR copied beside it. It is a scratch space for trying engine changes without a separate project; it ships empty.
.PP
.SH EDITOR SUPPORT
\fBCMAKE_EXPORT_COMPILE_COMMANDS ON\fR writes build/compile_commands.json; the \fB.clangd\fR file points clangd at it, so any editor with clangd (Neovim, VS Code, Emacs, Zed) gets completion and diagnostics for engine and SDL headers after the first configure.
.PP
.SH RELEASE CHECKLIST
.IP \(bu 2
Configure with \fB\-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release\fR; build; run once from a fresh terminal to confirm assets resolve.
.IP \(bu 2
Zip the executable and its \fBassets/\fR folder together; nothing else is needed.
.IP \(bu 2
On macOS, users may need to right\-click > Open the first time (unsigned binary). On Windows, MinGW builds are standalone; MSVC builds may need the VC redistributable.
.IP \(bu 2
Lowercase asset names, \fBorg\fR/\fBapp\fR set so saves go to the user folder, F3 overlay off by default (it is), version in the title or the title screen.
